<h2 id="1">A Hardcaml port of Niklaus Wirth's Project Oberon RISC5 machine runs DOOM at 15 fps</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-1">
<p>
Archive: <a href="https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/a-hardcaml-port-of-niklaus-wirths-project-oberon-risc5-machine-runs-doom-at-15-fps/18768/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/a-hardcaml-port-of-niklaus-wirths-project-oberon-risc5-machine-runs-doom-at-15-fps/18768/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org60914b2" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="org60914b2">zxygentoo announced</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-org60914b2">
<p>
<i>Most of this work was done a couple of months back; only recently did I find the time to tidy things up. (I drafted this write-up myself; Claude Opus checked and polished it as I'm not a native English speaker.)</i>
</p>

<p>
A Nexys 4 board with its LEDs lit, beside a monitor showing the Project Oberon desktop with DOOM's title screen in a viewer
</p>


<div id="org816ea5e" class="figure">
<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zxygentoo/oberon-risc-hardcaml/main/docs/oberon-on-nexys4.jpg" alt="oberon-on-nexys4.jpg" width="80%" />
</p>
</div>

<p>
I've always wanted to know more about Project Oberon, Wirth and Gutknecht's whole computer system (the CPU, the compiler, the OS), small enough for one person to understand, both its software and its hardware. With how far coding agents have come this year, there was really no reason to put it off any longer.
</p>

<p>
It started with a Rust port of Peter De Wachter's oberon-risc-emu, just to give myself an excuse to read the code, implement it in another language (with the aid of a coding agent, of course), and try out some testing techniques like lockstep simulation.
</p>

<p>
And things sort of got out of hand from there. Once the port worked, emulation wasn't enough for me, and if I was going to do it for real, Hardcaml was the obvious choice. So I bought a Nexys 4 FPGA dev board.
</p>

<p>
But first came yet another port of the emulator, in OCaml this time. That was out of necessity rather than aesthetics: as an OCaml library, the emulator runs in the same process as the Hardcaml simulator, so the two can be checked against each other in lockstep, instruction by instruction. Its <code>risc5_isa.mli</code>, the instruction type, later became what the DOOM compiler emits too.
</p>

<p>
The <code>/lib</code> folder holds the port of the machine, mostly unit for unit against Wirth's Verilog, and <code>/board/nexys-4</code> holds the board layer for the Nexys 4.
</p>

<p>
I'm new to this RTL thing, so a lot of thought went into verification:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>unit and fuzz tests for each unit (and some waveform tests, which double as nice documentation)</li>
<li>lockstep simulation against the emulator</li>
<li>co-simulation against the original Verilog with Verilator, unit by unit and the core over a whole boot</li>
<li>equivalence proofs against the original Verilog with yosys and z3 wherever it's doable, which turned out to be most of the units (17 proofs in all)</li>
</ul>

<p>
And, of course, it boots on real hardware, runs the Project Oberon image, and can rebuild the whole Oberon system on itself.
</p>

<p>
Hardcaml itself was a joy to work in. A circuit is just an OCaml function from one interface record to another (<code>[@@deriving hardcaml]</code> does the plumbing), every module has an <code>.mli</code> that owns its contract, and the tests sit next to the code as <code>ppx_expect</code> blocks, waveforms included, with QCheck for anything random. And because it's all just OCaml, the emulator, the simulator and the tests are one program, which makes the lockstep cheap.
</p>

<p>
Then I decided to have some fun with the machine. After all, a self-respecting operating system has to run DOOM, right? XD
</p>

<p>
The DOOM-on-Oberon project is not a C compiler for the RISC5 machine; it compiles just enough C for DOOM (by way of doomgeneric) to run:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>parse the C with goblint-cil (the Goblint project's fork of CIL) and lower its IR into RISC5 instructions (there are very few of them)</li>
<li>a hand-rolled mini libc, very crude register allocation, and a malloc that doesn't even free (DOOM brings its own zone allocator anyway)</li>
<li>DOOM's hot paths are well known (<code>R_DrawColumn</code>, <code>R_DrawSpan</code>, the dither, the frame copy), so I (well, the agent..) hand-wrote them in RISC5 assembly and they replace the compiled C at link time (this is the main <i>optimization</i> of the <i>compiler</i>, if it qualifies as one)</li>
<li>a <code>DOOM.Mod</code> Oberon module loads the compiled blob and the WAD, then calls <code>Tick</code> once per frame and <code>KeyIn</code> for each key press</li>
</ul>

<p>
And believe it or not, it ran, just not very fast: around 5 fps, so not playable.
</p>

<p>
I tried a bunch of different things, and basically only two worked well:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li><b>A bigger CPU cache.</b> The board already had a 4 KiB instruction cache to hide the PSRAM latency, which is plenty for Oberon, but DOOM's renderer doesn't fit in it. Growing it to 16 KiB got it to around 8 fps.</li>
<li><b>Dithering in hardware.</b> After that, more than half of each frame went into dithering DOOM's 8-bit colour onto Oberon's 1-bit screen. It was already hand-written, and I couldn't make it any faster in software, so the obvious choice was to move it into hardware. I mean, I do have an FPGA with resources to spare. So I added <code>halftone.mli</code>: an 8-bit display mode that dithers to monochrome at scan-out, with the tone table, threshold map and geometry all uploaded by the program. That got it to around 12 fps.</li>
</ol>

<p>
With a hand-rolled frame copy and the clock nudged from 60 to 64 MHz (the original runs at 25), DOOM's timedemo now runs at 15 fps. It plays fullscreen, or in a window on the live Oberon desktop (that's the photo above). I consider this mission accomplished.
</p>

<p>
On AI: agents were very helpful. Yes, they kind of like to write tasteless code, but if you take the time to set things up and lay down the initial patterns, they're pretty good at following them (with plenty of nagging, of course). And they make the tedious stuff tolerable: if I'd had to deal with Vivado through the GUI, I'd probably have quit halfway.
</p>

<p>
I intend to keep learning and maintaining both the RTL and the emulator projects, so issues and PRs are welcome.
</p>

<p>
All in all, a very fun project. Hardcaml is simple, powerful and elegant, and Project Oberon remains one of the most beautiful things in the history of computing. I hope I did it justice.
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>RTL: <a href="https://github.com/zxygentoo/oberon-risc-hardcaml">https://github.com/zxygentoo/oberon-risc-hardcaml</a></li>
<li>Emulator: <a href="https://github.com/zxygentoo/oberon-risc-emu-ocaml">https://github.com/zxygentoo/oberon-risc-emu-ocaml</a></li>
<li>DOOM: <a href="https://github.com/zxygentoo/DOOM-on-Oberon">https://github.com/zxygentoo/DOOM-on-Oberon</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
