<h2 id="2">mfetch, a simple tool to fetch your dependencies</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-2">
<p>
Archive: <a href="https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-mfetch-a-simple-tool-to-fetch-your-dependencies/18820/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-mfetch-a-simple-tool-to-fetch-your-dependencies/18820/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org5297227" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="org5297227">Calascibetta Romain announced</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-org5297227">
<p>
I am delighted to announce the release of <a href="https://github.com/robur-coop/mfetch"><code>mfetch.0.1.2</code></a>, a tool that allows you to download the source code for your dependencies and install them in a <code>vendors/</code> directory (as well as to populate the <code>dune</code> file at the root of your project with the <a href="https://dune.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/dune/vendored_dirs.html"><code>(vendored_dirs ...)</code> directive</a>). <code>mfetch</code> was developed to streamline the development of our unikernels (whilst still allowing us to verify their reproducibility).
</p>

<p>
The tool allows us to replace <a href="https://github.com/tarides/opam-monorepo"><code>opam-monorepo</code></a> (and eliminate the step of resolving version constraints, which OPAM already handles) and <code>opam source</code> (which is effective but limited in terms of source origins). In particular, it allows you to:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>download source code from your local drive, a Git repository (and create a Git repository using <code>git clone</code>) or directly from a published <span class="underline">tarball</span></li>
<li>track any changes the developer makes to this source code (see <code>mfetch status</code>)</li>
<li>and generate a lock file (in OPAM format) which helps verify the reproducibility of the targets using <a href="https://github.com/robur-coop/orb"><code>orb</code></a> (see <code>mfetch lock</code>)</li>
</ul>

<p>
For more details on how to use it, we recommend reading the <a href="https://github.com/robur-coop/mfetch/README.md"><code>README.md</code></a> and, with regard to unikernels, taking the time to read <a href="https://uniker.nl/unikernels-and-libraries/">our tutorial</a> on the subject. Although the project has a fairly specific focus, we would be delighted to expand it to include other use cases that are not necessarily limited to unikernels (one example being cross-compilation in general using the <code>dune-workspace</code> file).
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-3" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="3">Bootstrapping the OCaml compiler</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-3">
<p>
Archive: <a href="https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/bootstrapping-the-ocaml-compiler/18835/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/bootstrapping-the-ocaml-compiler/18835/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org817a9c4" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="org817a9c4">Vitor Sessak announced</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-org817a9c4">
<p>
Hi everyone!
</p>

<p>
I recently needed to import the OCaml compiler to our company's large internal monorepo. OCaml, like many compilers, uses a pre-compiled blob for bootstrapping.
</p>

<p>
I'm not a big fan of such binary blobs, so I prototyped a C++ OCaml interpreter that is expressive enough to bootstrap the compiler to fixpoint. Unlike camlboot, it is fast enough to become a common way of bootstrapping the compiler (it takes three minutes on my machine with 32 cores). It is not a real production OCaml interpreter, since it is much more permissive with types than a conforming interpreter should be. It uses the existing parsers from the <a href="https://github.com/tree-sitter">Tree-Sitter project</a> and the code was written with significant AI assistance, but the code quality is pretty acceptable.
</p>

<p>
Before I do a few more passes of improving the codebase, is there any interest in integrating this into the mainline OCaml compiler (either replacing the binary blobs or adding non-default support via optional configure flags)? The code can be found at <a href="https://github.com/vitor1001/ocaml/tree/bootstrap">https://github.com/vitor1001/ocaml/tree/bootstrap</a>.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-4" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="4">Dune 3.25</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-4">
<p>
Archive: <a href="https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-dune-3-25/18836/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-dune-3-25/18836/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org68bcb10" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="org68bcb10">Shon announced</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-org68bcb10">
<p>
The Dune team is pleased to announce <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/releases/tag/3.25.0">the release of dune 3.25.0</a>.
</p>

<p>
Highlights of this release include:
</p>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org8494a3e" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="org8494a3e">Breaking changes</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-org8494a3e">
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Actions no longer see the shared install staging area (<code>_build/install/&lt;context&gt;/{bin,lib,lib/stublibs}</code>) on <code>PATH</code>, <code>OCAMLPATH</code> and <code>CAML_LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code>. An action's environment only contains the packages it declares with <code>(deps (package &lt;pkg&gt;))</code> (and their library closure, see below). Actions that rely on packages being visible (such as tests that <code>#require</code> the package under test from an mdx block, steps that run <code>ocamlfind</code>, a nested <code>dune</code> build, or load a bytecode stub library) must now declare the package dependency explicitly. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/14373">ocaml/dune#14373</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/16617">ocaml/dune#16617</a>.</li>

<li>The <code>:</code> character is now rejected in context names, to ensure they remain portable path components. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/16085">ocaml/dune#16085</a>.</li>

<li>Generated files are now read-only even with Dune language versions before 2.4, regardless of whether the shared cache is enabled. Executable permissions are preserved. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/16388">ocaml/dune#16388</a></li>

<li>In dune-project, (warnings (duplicate_deps ..)) requires a dune lang version of 3.22, instead of 3.18.1. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/5696">ocaml/dune#5696</a>.</li>

<li><code>(env (env-vars ...))</code> in a <code>(context ...)</code> stanza of <code>dune-workspace</code> now takes precedence over the root <code>env</code> stanza, as documented. This affects commands such as <code>dune exec</code>; rules already used the documented order. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/16290">ocaml/dune#16290</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-org1aa7b00" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="org1aa7b00">Features</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-org1aa7b00">
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Added command, subcommand, and flag completion in the command line. Supported shells are <code>bash</code>, <code>zsh</code>, <code>pwsh</code>, and <code>fish</code>. See <code>dune completion [shell] --help</code>. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/14779">ocaml/dune#14779</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15334">ocaml/dune#15334</a>.</li>

<li>Tab completion for <code>dune promote</code> now lists pending promotion files. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/5338">ocaml/dune#5338</a>.</li>

<li>Add build time information to watch mode. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15384">ocaml/dune#15384</a>.</li>

<li>Display build duration and average process parallelism in the status line for batch builds. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15564">ocaml/dune#15564</a>.</li>

<li>Add <code>(flags ...)</code> to the <code>ocamllex</code> and <code>ocamlyacc</code> stanzas to pass custom options to parser generators. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15907">ocaml/dune#15907</a>.</li>

<li>Starting with Dune language 3.25, restrict sandboxed actions on Linux systems with Landlock ABI 3 or newer from writing outside their sandbox, temporary directory, action-trace directory, and <code>/dev</code>. Set <code>DUNE_CONFIG__LANDLOCK=disabled</code> to opt out. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15858">ocaml/dune#15858</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-orgf7b22be" class="outline-4">
<h4 id="orgf7b22be">Fixes</h4>
<div class="outline-text-4" id="text-orgf7b22be">
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><code>$ dune rpc build</code> invocations no longer queue. All build requests are built in parallel. Watch servers started by <code>$ dune exec --watch</code> can now also handle forwarded build and runtest requests. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/14954">ocaml/dune#14954</a>. <code>dune rpc build</code> also now exits with the proper code on completion. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15341">ocaml/dune#15341</a>.</li>

<li>Portable lock directories now select one package version across all requested platforms, and fail when no common version exists. <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/pull/15982">ocaml/dune#15982</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>
&mdash;
</p>

<p>
See <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/releases/tag/3.25.0">the full changelog</a> for all new features and fixes, and for attribution to the contributors who made it all possible. Thank you, contributors!
</p>

<p>
If you encounter a problem with this release, please report it in <a href="https://github.com/ocaml/dune/issues">our issue tracker</a>.
</p>
</div>
</div>
